---
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';
const site = 'https://www.ewohub.com';
---

<Base
  title="Why Your Webcam Room Has Low Viewers — 8 Real Reasons (and Fixes)"
  description="Low viewer counts on cam platforms are frustrating but almost always fixable. Here are the 8 most common reasons webcam models get low viewers — and what to do about each one."
  lang="en"
  hreflang={[
    { lang: 'en', url: `${site}/why-low-viewers-webcam` },
    { lang: 'de', url: `${site}/de/why-low-viewers-webcam` },
    { lang: 'es', url: `${site}/es/why-low-viewers-webcam` },
    { lang: 'ro', url: `${site}/ro/why-low-viewers-webcam` },
    { lang: 'uk', url: `${site}/uk/why-low-viewers-webcam` },
    { lang: 'ru', url: `${site}/ru/why-low-viewers-webcam` },
  ]}
>

<!-- HERO -->
<div class="art-hero">
  <div class="art-orb art-orb--1"></div>
  <div class="art-orb art-orb--2"></div>
  <div class="art-grid"></div>
  <div class="art-hero__inner container">
    <div class="art-meta">
      <span class="art-tag">
        <svg width="10" height="10" viewBox="0 0 10 10" fill="none"><circle cx="5" cy="5" r="4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/><path d="M5 3v2l1.5 1.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
        8 min read
      </span>
      <span class="art-tag">Guide</span>
      <span class="art-tag art-tag--green">
        <span class="dot-green"></span>
        Actionable fixes
      </span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="art-title">Why Your Webcam Room<br><span class="art-grad">Has Low Viewers</span></h1>
    <p class="art-lead">Low viewer counts are frustrating but almost always fixable. Here are the 8 most common reasons webcam models get low viewers — and exactly what to do about each one.</p>
    <div class="art-stats-row">
      <div class="art-stat-item">
        <span class="art-stat-num">8</span>
        <span class="art-stat-lab">Root causes</span>
      </div>
      <div class="art-stat-div"></div>
      <div class="art-stat-item">
        <span class="art-stat-num">4–6w</span>
        <span class="art-stat-lab">Avg. time to fix</span>
      </div>
      <div class="art-stat-div"></div>
      <div class="art-stat-item">
        <span class="art-stat-num">10×</span>
        <span class="art-stat-lab">Potential viewer growth</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- BODY -->
<div class="art-body container">
  <div class="art-layout">

    <!-- TOC sidebar -->
    <aside class="art-toc">
      <p class="art-toc__title">Contents</p>
      <ol class="art-toc__list">
        <li><a href="#reason-1">Wrong streaming time</a></li>
        <li><a href="#reason-2">Wrong tags & category</a></li>
        <li><a href="#reason-3">Bad preview thumbnail</a></li>
        <li><a href="#reason-4">No off-platform presence</a></li>
        <li><a href="#reason-5">Inconsistent schedule</a></li>
        <li><a href="#reason-6">Low room engagement</a></li>
        <li><a href="#reason-7">Algorithm not calibrated</a></li>
        <li><a href="#reason-8">Saturated niche</a></li>
      </ol>
      <a href="#cta" class="art-toc__cta">Get a free audit →</a>
    </aside>

    <!-- Main content -->
    <main class="art-main">

      <p class="art-intro">This is especially common on high-competition platforms like <strong>Chaturbate</strong> and <strong>Stripchat</strong>, where promotion and visibility — not just content quality — determine how many viewers actually find your room.</p>

      <!-- REASON 1 -->
      <div class="reason-card" id="reason-1">
        <div class="reason-num">01</div>
        <div class="reason-content">
          <h2>You're Streaming at the Wrong Time</h2>
          <p>This is the single most common and most impactful mistake new models make. Cam platforms have predictable traffic peaks that vary significantly by region and day of week. Streaming during off-peak hours means you're competing for a fraction of the available active viewers — and the platform algorithm has less traffic to push your room into.</p>
          <p>Most new models stream when it's convenient for them personally. The viewers they're trying to reach are often in a completely different time zone. A model in Eastern Europe targeting North American viewers who streams at 8pm local time is streaming at 1pm Eastern — well before the US peak traffic window.</p>

          <div class="pull-quote">
            <svg class="pull-quote__icon" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M3 21c3 0 7-1 7-8V5c0-1.25-.756-2.017-2-2H4c-1.25 0-2 .75-2 1.972V11c0 1.25.75 2 2 2 1 0 1 0 1 1v1c0 1-1 2-2 2s-1 .008-1 1.031V20c0 1 0 1 1 1z" fill="currentColor" opacity=".15"/><path d="M15 21c3 0 7-1 7-8V5c0-1.25-.757-2.017-2-2h-4c-1.25 0-2 .75-2 1.972V11c0 1.25.75 2 2 2h.75c0 2.25.25 4-2.75 4v3c0 1 0 1 1 1z" fill="currentColor" opacity=".15"/><path d="M3 21c3 0 7-1 7-8V5c0-1.25-.756-2.017-2-2H4c-1.25 0-2 .75-2 1.972V11c0 1.25.75 2 2 2 1 0 1 0 1 1v1c0 1-1 2-2 2s-1 .008-1 1.031V20c0 1 0 1 1 1zm12 0c3 0 7-1 7-8V5c0-1.25-.757-2.017-2-2h-4c-1.25 0-2 .75-2 1.972V11c0 1.25.75 2 2 2h.75c0 2.25.25 4-2.75 4v3c0 1 0 1 1 1z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
            Streaming at the wrong time can reduce your effective audience by <strong>10×</strong> compared to peak hours — even with an identical setup.
          </div>

          <div class="fix-box">
            <div class="fix-box__header">
              <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
              Fix
            </div>
            <p>Identify your target audience's region and build your schedule around their peak hours. See our <a href="/best-streaming-times-by-region">streaming times by region guide</a> for specific windows per country.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- REASON 2 -->
      <div class="reason-card" id="reason-2">
        <div class="reason-num">02</div>
        <div class="reason-content">
          <h2>Your Tags and Category Are Wrong</h2>
          <p>Platform search works almost entirely on tags. If you're using generic, highly competitive tags — the ones every model in your category uses — you're effectively invisible in search. You're competing with thousands of other rooms for the same keyword, and the algorithm will show established rooms with proven viewer history before yours.</p>
          <p>The opposite problem is equally common: tags that are too specific or too unusual for anyone to search for. Neither extreme works.</p>

          <div class="two-col-box">
            <div class="two-col-box__item two-col-box__item--bad">
              <p class="two-col-box__label">❌ Avoid</p>
              <p>Generic tags with 10,000+ competing rooms — you'll never rank in these</p>
            </div>
            <div class="two-col-box__item two-col-box__item--good">
              <p class="two-col-box__label">✓ Better</p>
              <p>Mid-competition tags with 500–2,000 rooms — visible and searchable</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="fix-box">
            <div class="fix-box__header">
              <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
              Fix
            </div>
            <p>Research which tags in your category have moderate competition — specific enough to be findable, common enough that viewers actually search them. Your category selection also matters: some categories have far less competition for the same type of content.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- REASON 3 -->
      <div class="reason-card" id="reason-3">
        <div class="reason-num">03</div>
        <div class="reason-content">
          <h2>Your Preview Thumbnail Isn't Converting</h2>
          <p>On most cam platforms, viewers browse through a grid of room thumbnails before clicking into any room. Your thumbnail — the preview image or live snapshot — is often the <em>only thing</em> a viewer sees before deciding whether to click.</p>

          <div class="checklist">
            <p class="checklist__title">Common thumbnail problems:</p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M15 9l-6 6M9 9l6 6" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
                Bad lighting — room looks dark and unprofessional
              </li>
              <li>
                <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M15 9l-6 6M9 9l6 6" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
                Unflattering or low camera angle
              </li>
              <li>
                <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M15 9l-6 6M9 9l6 6" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
                Visually cluttered or distracting background
              </li>
              <li>
                <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M15 9l-6 6M9 9l6 6" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
                Expression that reads as bored or uncomfortable
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="fix-box">
            <div class="fix-box__header">
              <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
              Fix
            </div>
            <p>Invest in basic lighting — even an inexpensive ring light dramatically changes how thumbnails look. Set your camera angle intentionally. Check how your room appears in the platform's category grid <em>before</em> each session and adjust if needed.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- REASON 4 -->
      <div class="reason-card" id="reason-4">
        <div class="reason-num">04</div>
        <div class="reason-content">
          <h2>You Have No Off-Platform Presence</h2>
          <p>Models who rely entirely on the platform algorithm for discovery have no floor on their viewership — every session starts from zero. Models with off-platform followings have a base of viewers who find out about their streams independently of platform ranking.</p>

          <div class="highlight-box">
            <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M13 2L3 14h9l-1 8 10-12h-9l1-8z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
            <p>Even a small off-platform following of <strong>200–500 followers</strong> creates a meaningful difference in your first-viewer arrival — which in turn improves your algorithmic ranking from the start of each stream.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="fix-box">
            <div class="fix-box__header">
              <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
              Fix
            </div>
            <p>Build at minimum a Twitter/X presence with consistent posting that includes when you're going live. Twitter/X and Reddit are the two most important off-platform channels for most cam models — both have active communities of platform viewers.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- REASON 5 -->
      <div class="reason-card" id="reason-5">
        <div class="reason-num">05</div>
        <div class="reason-content">
          <h2>You're Not Streaming Consistently</h2>
          <p>Cam platform algorithms have a memory. Models who stream on a consistent schedule train both the algorithm and their audience. The algorithm learns when to expect your room and can pre-warm its ranking. Viewers learn to plan for your streams.</p>
          <p>Models who stream irregularly — different times, different days, unpredictable gaps — get treated by the algorithm as less reliable performers.</p>

          <div class="pull-quote">
            <svg class="pull-quote__icon" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M3 21c3 0 7-1 7-8V5c0-1.25-.756-2.017-2-2H4c-1.25 0-2 .75-2 1.972V11c0 1.25.75 2 2 2 1 0 1 0 1 1v1c0 1-1 2-2 2s-1 .008-1 1.031V20c0 1 0 1 1 1z" fill="currentColor" opacity=".15"/><path d="M15 21c3 0 7-1 7-8V5c0-1.25-.757-2.017-2-2h-4c-1.25 0-2 .75-2 1.972V11c0 1.25.75 2 2 2h.75c0 2.25.25 4-2.75 4v3c0 1 0 1 1 1z" fill="currentColor" opacity=".15"/><path d="M3 21c3 0 7-1 7-8V5c0-1.25-.756-2.017-2-2H4c-1.25 0-2 .75-2 1.972V11c0 1.25.75 2 2 2 1 0 1 0 1 1v1c0 1-1 2-2 2s-1 .008-1 1.031V20c0 1 0 1 1 1zm12 0c3 0 7-1 7-8V5c0-1.25-.757-2.017-2-2h-4c-1.25 0-2 .75-2 1.972V11c0 1.25.75 2 2 2h.75c0 2.25.25 4-2.75 4v3c0 1 0 1 1 1z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
            Consistency compounds — viewers who know when you're live start planning their week around it. Returning viewers are the foundation of sustainable cam income.
          </div>

          <div class="fix-box">
            <div class="fix-box__header">
              <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
              Fix
            </div>
            <p>Pick <strong>3–5 streaming days per week</strong> and treat them as fixed commitments. Announce your schedule on your profile and on social media.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- REASON 6 -->
      <div class="reason-card" id="reason-6">
        <div class="reason-num">06</div>
        <div class="reason-content">
          <h2>Your Room Engagement Is Low</h2>
          <p>Most cam platforms factor room engagement — chat activity, tip frequency, viewer interaction — into how they rank rooms. A room where nobody talks, nobody tips, and the model doesn't engage with chat signals low quality content to the algorithm, even if the model is objectively good.</p>

          <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--warning">
            <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M10.29 3.86L1.82 18a2 2 0 001.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 001.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 00-3.42 0z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="12" y1="9" x2="12" y2="13" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round"/><line x1="12" y1="17" x2="12.01" y2="17" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
            <p>This creates a frustrating catch-22: you need engagement to rank, but you need ranking to get viewers to engage. Breaking out requires actively creating engagement even with small audiences.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="fix-box">
            <div class="fix-box__header">
              <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
              Fix
            </div>
            <p>Treat your chat as a community, not a side window. Respond to every message when you're small. Ask questions. Create small interactive moments — polls, challenges, decisions viewers can vote on. Even minimal engagement from 3–4 viewers looks very different to the algorithm than 10 silent lurkers.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- REASON 7 -->
      <div class="reason-card" id="reason-7">
        <div class="reason-num">07</div>
        <div class="reason-content">
          <h2>You're New — Algorithm Hasn't Calibrated Yet</h2>
          <p>Platform algorithms take time to understand a new account. In the first <strong>4–8 weeks</strong> of consistent streaming, the algorithm is learning: who watches you, how long they stay, what they respond to, which audience segments engage most.</p>
          <p>Most models interpret this period as evidence they're failing — and either quit or switch strategy before the algorithm has had time to calibrate. <em>This is the #1 mistake new models make.</em></p>

          <div class="fix-box">
            <div class="fix-box__header">
              <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
              Fix
            </div>
            <p>Give the algorithm at least <strong>6–8 weeks</strong> of consistent, strategic streaming before drawing conclusions. Track your metrics week-over-week, not day-over-day. Early trends are noisy; the pattern over 6+ weeks tells you the real story.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- REASON 8 -->
      <div class="reason-card" id="reason-8">
        <div class="reason-num">08</div>
        <div class="reason-content">
          <h2>You're in a Saturated Niche Without Differentiation</h2>
          <p>Some cam categories have thousands of models streaming simultaneously at peak hours. In a category that crowded, a new model with no established audience and no clear differentiation has virtually no reason to attract viewers over established performers.</p>
          <p>"Being good" isn't a differentiator because every model in the category is already good enough. Viewers choose rooms based on identity, vibe, community, and consistency — not raw performance quality.</p>

          <div class="pull-quote">
            <svg class="pull-quote__icon" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M3 21c3 0 7-1 7-8V5c0-1.25-.756-2.017-2-2H4c-1.25 0-2 .75-2 1.972V11c0 1.25.75 2 2 2 1 0 1 0 1 1v1c0 1-1 2-2 2s-1 .008-1 1.031V20c0 1 0 1 1 1z" fill="currentColor" opacity=".15"/><path d="M15 21c3 0 7-1 7-8V5c0-1.25-.757-2.017-2-2h-4c-1.25 0-2 .75-2 1.972V11c0 1.25.75 2 2 2h.75c0 2.25.25 4-2.75 4v3c0 1 0 1 1 1z" fill="currentColor" opacity=".15"/><path d="M3 21c3 0 7-1 7-8V5c0-1.25-.756-2.017-2-2H4c-1.25 0-2 .75-2 1.972V11c0 1.25.75 2 2 2 1 0 1 0 1 1v1c0 1-1 2-2 2s-1 .008-1 1.031V20c0 1 0 1 1 1zm12 0c3 0 7-1 7-8V5c0-1.25-.757-2.017-2-2h-4c-1.25 0-2 .75-2 1.972V11c0 1.25.75 2 2 2h.75c0 2.25.25 4-2.75 4v3c0 1 0 1 1 1z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
            A niche within a niche almost always outperforms generic positioning in crowded categories.
          </div>

          <div class="fix-box">
            <div class="fix-box__header">
              <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
              Fix
            </div>
            <p>Define what makes your room specifically worth choosing. This doesn't require being a completely different type of performer — it requires a clear personality, aesthetic, or community identity that viewers can recognize and return to.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- SUMMARY -->
      <div class="summary-card">
        <div class="summary-card__icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M9 11l3 3L22 4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M21 12v7a2 2 0 01-2 2H5a2 2 0 01-2-2V5a2 2 0 012-2h11" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2>The Common Thread</h2>
          <p>Almost all low-viewer problems come back to the same root cause: <strong>streaming without a strategy</strong>. Good content is necessary but not sufficient. The models who consistently grow an audience treat streaming as a business — with a schedule, a positioning strategy, an off-platform presence, and regular review of what's actually working.</p>
          <p style="margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;">If you've identified multiple issues from the list above, start with <strong>timing and profile optimization</strong> — they have the highest leverage and lowest effort. Then build from there.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section class="faq-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>
            How long does it take to fix a low-viewer problem?
            <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
          </summary>
          <p>Timing fixes and profile fixes can show results within 1–2 weeks. Building an off-platform presence takes 4–8 weeks to become meaningful. Algorithmic calibration takes 6–8 weeks of consistent streaming. Most models who apply all fixes simultaneously see clear improvement within <strong>4–6 weeks</strong>.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>
            Does the time of day really matter that much?
            <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
          </summary>
          <p>Yes — streaming during peak hours vs. off-peak hours can mean the difference between competing for 10,000 active viewers and competing for 1,000. At small audience sizes, this difference is often the entire margin between getting viewers and getting none.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>
            I have viewers but they never tip. Is that a different problem?
            <svg class="faq-chevron" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
          </summary>
          <p>Usually, yes. Low viewers and low tips have partially overlapping but different causes. Tipping behavior relates more to community development, audience loyalty, and in-stream engagement techniques than to the discovery factors that drive raw viewer count.</p>
        </details>
      </section>

      <!-- CTA -->
      <div class="art-cta" id="cta">
        <div class="art-cta__orb"></div>
        <div class="art-cta__inner">
          <div class="art-cta__badge">Free audit</div>
          <h2>Want Help Diagnosing Your Specific Situation?</h2>
          <p>We offer free profile audits as part of our onboarding process. Send us your profile and current streaming situation and we'll identify the highest-leverage fixes for your specific case.</p>
          <div class="art-cta__actions">
            <a href="https://t.me/+tBjfmjh9vHo1YzQy" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="art-cta__btn art-cta__btn--primary">
              <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M21 2L2 9l7 4 2 7 10-18z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
              Get a free profile audit
            </a>
            <a href="/model-promotion" class="art-cta__btn art-cta__btn--ghost">See promotion services →</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- RELATED -->
      <section class="related-section">
        <h2>Related Articles</h2>
        <div class="related-grid">
          <a href="/raise-your-room-ranking-score" class="related-card">
            <div class="related-card__icon">
              <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><polyline points="22 12 18 12 15 21 9 3 6 12 2 12" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
            </div>
            <p>How to Raise Your Room's Ranking Score</p>
            <span>Read →</span>
          </a>
          <a href="/how-cam-algorithm-ranks-rooms" class="related-card">
            <div class="related-card__icon">
              <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M19.07 4.93a10 10 0 010 14.14M4.93 4.93a10 10 0 000 14.14" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
